<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna GH-275: a whole-width write to a register that holds an ABI parameter
    or return entry loses the argument and the return value at the call.

    Heritage characterizes every heritaged range against each call site's
    prototype.  When the range is strictly LARGER than the ABI storage inside
    it, the characterization is ContainedBy, and upstream Ghidra runs two
    dedicated guards: Heritage::guardCallOverlappingInput (heritage.cc:1210) on
    the input side, and Heritage::tryOutputOverlapGuard with
    Heritage::guardOutputOverlap (heritage.cc:1249 and :1293) on the output
    side.  kuna shipped BOTH as comment-only stubs, so the parameter and return
    entries inside such a range were invisible to trial recovery.

    That range shape is routine on x86-64: the vendored ia.sinc models PXOR as
    one 128-bit write to the whole XmmReg (XmmReg = XmmReg ^ m128), so the
    refinement gate in Heritage::refinement (size above 4 bytes AND the widest
    write narrower than the range) never fires, and the XMM0 range stays 16
    bytes wide while the SysV entry inside it is 8.

    The vector is gcc -O2 output from five lines of plain C, with no intrinsics,
    no SIMD in the source and no libm, because the trigger is the compiler's
    canonical long-to-double idiom rather than packed arithmetic:

        double u(double a, double b);
        double sink;
        double f(long n, double b) { double r = u((double)n, b); sink = r; return r + 1.0; }

    Bytes of f at 0x1140 (47 bytes, extracted from the built binary):
        1140  f3 0f 1e fa               ENDBR64
        1144  66 0f 28 c8               MOVAPD   XMM1,XMM0   ; 2 x 8-byte lanes, XMM1 refines
        1148  66 0f ef c0               PXOR     XMM0,XMM0   ; ONE 16-byte write, XMM0 never refines
        114c  48 83 ec 08               SUB      RSP,0x8
        1150  f2 48 0f 2a c7            CVTSI2SD XMM0,RDI    ; the argument the call loses
        1155  e8 16 00 00 00            CALL     0x1170
        115a  f2 0f 11 05 b6 2e 00 00   MOVSD    [0x4018],XMM0   ; the return value the call loses
        1162  f2 0f 58 05 9e 0e 00 00   ADDSD    XMM0,[0x2008]
        116a  48 83 c4 08               ADD      RSP,0x8
        116e  c3                        RET

    0x1170 is a bare RET and carries no symbol on purpose: a locked callee
    prototype makes FuncCallSpecs::isInputActive false and bypasses the whole
    branch, which is why the defect is invisible on declared callees.  0x2008
    holds 1.0 and 0x4018 is the global the source calls sink.

    Stage: P3 heritage call guarding, option calloverlap off|in|full.  Pass 1
    (off) pins the bug, pass 2 (full) pins the completed upstream guards.
    Ghidra 11.4.3 on this binary emits
        double f(long param_1) { sink = (double)u((double)param_1); return sink + _DAT_00102008; }
    which pass 2 matches exactly, modulo the names this stripped bytechunk has
    no symbols for.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1140">
f30f1efa660f28c8660fefc04883ec08f2480f2ac7e816000000f20f1105b62e0000f20f58059e0e00004883c408c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1170">
c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x2008" readonly="true">
000000000000f03f
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x4018">
0000000000000000
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1140" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option calloverlap off</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option calloverlap full</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="CALLOVERLAP #1: off, the call is emitted with no arguments at all (the CVTSI2SD result never reaches it)" min="1" max="1">sub_1170\(\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLOVERLAP #2: off, f loses its own parameter list because the argument chain dies with the trial" min="1" max="1">float8 f\(void\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLOVERLAP #3: off, the call result is read from a local that nothing ever assigns" min="1" max="1">dat_4018 = v1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLOVERLAP #4: full, guardCallOverlappingInput recovers the truncated XMM0 argument and tryOutputOverlapGuard the return value" min="1" max="1">dat_4018 = \(float8\)sub_1170\(\(float8\)a0\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLOVERLAP #5: full, f regains its own parameter" min="1" max="1">float8 f\(int8 a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLOVERLAP #6: full, the epilogue adds 1.0 to the recovered call result, not to a stale pre-call read" min="1" max="1">return dat_4018 \+ dat_2008;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
